course: biology
lesson: 294
title: Cats through history: Egypt's sacred cats, ship cats and Tom and Jerry
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Nobody really sat down to tame the cat. About 10,000 years ago, when the first farmers in the Middle East stored grain, mice moved in, and wild cats followed the mice. People let the useful hunters stay, so in a way cats tamed themselves.
explain: In ancient Egypt cats kept mice and snakes away from food and homes and became sacred: the goddess Bastet had a cat's head, and millions of cats were mummified as gifts to the gods.
explain: Later, cats sailed on ships to catch rats, and that is how they spread around the world. Today they curl up on our sofas and purr.
explain: Cartoons get cats wrong in funny ways. Tom never catches Jerry, cartoon cats lap saucers of milk, and they always land on their feet. Real cats usually catch the mouse, milk upsets most grown-up cats' tummies, and a fall from a window can badly hurt them.
fact: Wild cats came to the first farming villages in the Middle East about 10,000 years ago to hunt mice in the grain stores, and people let them stay.
fact: The oldest known cat buried with a person was found in Cyprus and is about 9,500 years old.
fact: Ancient Egyptians loved cats, which kept mice and snakes away; the goddess Bastet had a cat's head, and killing a cat could bring a death sentence.
fact: Millions of cat mummies were made as gifts to the gods; in the 1800s so many were found that some were ground up as fertiliser, and scientists now X-ray them.
fact: Real cats do hunt mice and often play with them first, which is practice; real mice cannot outsmart a cat with traps and hammers as Jerry does.
fact: Cats jump at a cucumber placed silently behind them because something new and snake-like has suddenly appeared, and scaring pets on purpose is unkind.
fact: Cats have a righting reflex that twists them in the air, but they can still be badly hurt in falls, and vets see many cats that fell from windows; cats do not have nine lives.
fact: Most adult cats cannot digest milk sugar well and can get an upset tummy; water is the best drink, and shop cat milk has the milk sugar removed.
fact: In medieval Europe some people linked black cats with witches, but in Britain and Japan black cats can mean good luck; it is only superstition.
fact: Ships carried cats to catch rats that ate food and ropes, sailors thought cats brought luck, and cats spread around the world on ships.
fact: Pet cats that go outside kill many wild birds and small animals, and in Australia cats are a big threat to native animals; a bell on the collar and keeping cats in at night help.
fact: Cats purr when happy but also when hurt or scared, to calm themselves; the purr is made by muscles in the voice box, and lions cannot purr but can roar.
fact: Cats sleep about 12 to 16 hours a day, because hunters save energy between short bursts of action, and lions also sleep most of the day.
exercise: Why did wild cats first move into farming villages? | To hunt the mice in the grain stores | To be fed warm milk by the farmers every day | To keep warm by the fires | To guard the sheep from wolves at night
exercise: Why do people say cats tamed themselves? | They came to the villages on their own, and people let the useful hunters stay | Cats trained each other in tricks until people wanted them as pets | Cats built their own houses | A king ordered all cats to become tame, and they obeyed him
exercise: Where was the oldest known cat buried with a person? | Cyprus | Egypt | China | France
exercise: Why did ancient Egyptians value cats? | They kept mice and snakes away from food and homes | They pulled carts full of stone for the pyramids | They laid eggs | They gave milk and cheese
exercise: Which Egyptian goddess had a cat's head? | Bastet | Athena | Freya | Venus
exercise: Why were millions of cats mummified in Egypt? | As gifts to the gods | To keep as toys | To sell as food | To stop them running away
exercise: Why does a real cat often play with a mouse before killing it? | It is practising its hunting | It wants to be friends | It is scared of the mouse | It is waiting for the mouse to grow
exercise: Why do cats jump away from a cucumber behind them? | Something new and snake-like has silently appeared | Cats hate the colour green more than any other colour | Cucumbers smell of dogs | Cats are allergic to all vegetables and can feel it
exercise: What twists a falling cat the right way up? | A righting reflex | Its tail acting like a parachute | Its whiskers catching the wind | Its claws gripping the air
exercise: Can a cat be badly hurt falling from a window? | Yes, vets see many cats hurt in falls | No, cats always land safely on their feet | No, cats have nine lives, so a fall cannot harm them | Only black cats get hurt
exercise: What is the best drink for a cat? | Water | Cow's milk | Fizzy lemonade | Cold tea
exercise: Why does a saucer of cow's milk often upset a grown-up cat? | Most adult cats cannot digest milk sugar well | Milk is too cold for cats | Milk makes their fur fall out in big clumps | Cats are scared of anything white, and the fright upsets them
exercise: Where can black cats mean good luck? | In Britain and Japan | Nowhere in the world | Only in medieval Europe | Only on Fridays
exercise: Why did ships carry cats? | To catch rats that ate food and ropes | To help steer the ship | To catch fresh fish for the crew to eat | To keep the captain warm in his bunk on cold nights
exercise: What helps protect wild birds from pet cats? | A bell on the collar and keeping cats in at night | Feeding cats only fish | Giving cats lots of milk so they are never hungry | Letting cats out only at night, when birds are asleep
exercise: When do cats purr? | When happy, but also when hurt or scared | Only when happy and being stroked | Only when hungry | Only when asleep and dreaming of mice
exercise: Why do cats sleep 12 to 16 hours a day? | Hunters save energy between short bursts of action | They are always ill | They are bored of people and have nothing else to do | They cannot see at all in the daytime
test: In Tom and Jerry, Jerry hits Tom with a frying pan and gets away every time. What is real? | A real cat usually catches the mouse, as mice cannot fight back like that | Mice always beat cats, because they are much cleverer | Cats are afraid of mice | Mice and cats never meet in real life, they live apart
test: «Tom laps a saucer of milk, so I'll give our cat one too» What is better? | Give water; milk can upset most grown-up cats' tummies | Milk is the best drink for cats, just like in the cartoon | Give her lemonade instead | Give her nothing to drink at all, cats get water from food
test: «cat jumped at a bird off the balcony, he'll be fine, they always land on their feet» What is right? | No, cats can be badly hurt in falls | Yes, cats never get hurt | Yes, cats have nine lives, so he's got eight more | Only if he is a black cat
test: «saw a vid, cucumber behind a cat, it freaked lol, gonna try it» What is right? | It frightens the cat, and scaring pets on purpose is unkind | It is a fun game that cats really enjoy playing again and again | Cats like cucumbers to eat | It does nothing to a cat
test: «black cat crossed my path, worst day ever coming» What is right? | It is only superstition; in Britain and Japan black cats can mean good luck | Black cats always bring bad luck, so it's best to go home and stay in bed | Black cats are witches | Only black cats cause accidents
test: Count them: 4 cats sit on a ship's deck. How many legs, tails and whiskers if each has 24? | 16 legs, 4 tails and 96 whiskers | 16 legs, 4 tails and 24 whiskers | 12 legs, 4 tails and 96 whiskers | 16 legs, 8 tails and 96 whiskers
test: Count them: a cat has 18 claws, 5 on each front paw and 4 on each back paw. How many claws do 3 cats have? | 54 | 48 | 60 | 36
test: «our cat purrs, so she's fine, even though she's limping» What is right? | Cats purr when hurt too, so she may need a vet | Purring means she cannot be hurt, so the limp is nothing | Limping cats never purr | Purring heals any injury
test: «Garfield's lazy, all cats are lazy, sleep all day» What is right? | Cats sleep a lot to save energy for short bursts of hunting | Cats are lazy because they are ill | Cats sleep because they are sad and lonely without people around | Only cartoon cats sleep
test: What would happen to a ship's food store long ago with no cat and no traps? | Rats would eat the food and ropes | The food would last longer | Rats would leave the ship at once and swim off to land | Nothing, ships had no rats
test: «why'd anyone grind up thousands of cat mummies» What happened in the 1800s? | So many were found that some were used as fertiliser | They were made into medicine for sick cats and dogs | They were sold as dolls | It never happened, it's a myth
test: Why do some places in Australia ask cat owners to keep cats inside? | Cats are a big threat to native birds and small animals there | Australian cats are dangerous to people and often attack | Kangaroos chase cats and kick them | Cats there are too hot outside in the burning summer sun
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the domestication of the cat. | Wild cats were drawn to early farming settlements by rodents and were tolerated there | Cats were deliberately captured and bred by Egyptian kings from the outset | Cats were domesticated before the dog | Cats descended from the lion, which was gradually reduced in size by selective breeding
exam: Identify the drink most suitable for an adult domestic cat. | Water | Cow's milk | Cream | Sweetened tea
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the purr of the domestic cat? | It may signify contentment but may equally accompany pain or fear | It signifies contentment exclusively | It is produced by the tail | It is shared by all large cats, including the lion and the tiger
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which cats were carried aboard ships. | The destruction of rats that consumed provisions and rope | The navigation of the vessel by night | The provision of meat for the crew | The prediction of storms
